<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahabad</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saujbulagh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahabad is a city in West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> province in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-urmia">Lake Urmia</a>, with a mainly Kurdish population. Formerly called Saujbulagh, it took the name Mahabad in the 1930s. In 1946 it was the capital of the Soviet-backed Republic of Mahabad, the first modern Kurdish republic, which collapsed when Iranian troops returned in December 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahabad.jpg" alt="Mahabad" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simko at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahabad-Dec_2006.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mahabad lies south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-urmia">Lake Urmia</a> in a narrow valley 1,300 metres above sea level, and is the capital of Mahabad County in West Azerbaijan province. By road it is 122 kilometres from the provincial capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urmia">Urmia</a> and 679 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>. Most of its people are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> who follow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a>, and many also speak Persian and Azeri Turkic. Neo-Aramaic-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> once lived in the city as well. Because of its place in Kurdish nationalism, the Unrepresented Nations and Peoples Organization treats it as the capital of Iranian Kurdistan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city's older name, Savojbolagh, is a Persian corruption of the Turkic soghuk bulak, meaning cold spring; the Kurdish form was Sablagh. It is first attested in the 16th century, in the Safavid era, when the Mukri Kurds took part in the wars between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and gained prominence. In the 17th century it became the seat of the Mukri principality. Many believe that Budaq Sultan Mukri, who built the congregational mosque, founded the present town. The name Mahabad came into use after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah</a>, who ruled from 1925 to 1941.</p><p>Mahabad was the capital of the short-lived Republic of Mahabad, declared on 1 January 1946 under the Kurdish nationalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qazi-muhammad">Qazi Muhammad</a>. It had strong Soviet support, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> had occupied northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and it included the mainly Kurdish-speaking towns of Bukan, Piranshahr, Sardasht and Oshnavieh. After an agreement brokered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the Soviets agreed to leave Iran. The Shah's government then moved against the republic, and its leaders were arrested. Qazi Muhammad was hanged on 31 March 1947.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2016 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 168,393 people in the city. The Mahabad dialect is the literary standard of Kurdish in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and is very close to the Sorani used in Iraqi Kurdistan. Several poets and writers came from the city, among them Hejar (Abdurrahman Sharafkandi), who wrote the first Kurdish-Kurdish-Persian dictionary in Iran, and Hemin, and both were given the title of Kurdistan's national poet under the republic. The translator Mohammad Ghazi, who rendered more than 70 literary works into Persian, was also from Mahabad.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahabad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1946 the poem was adopted as the national anthem of the Kurdistan Republic of <b>Mahabad</b>, a short-lived Kurdish republic in Iran that lasted for a year.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ey-reqib">Ey Reqîb</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Soviet occupation of northern Iran, Kurdish nationalists in <b>Mahabad</b> formed the Komala society and then the Kurdistan Democratic Party under Qazi Muhammad, a respected judge and religious leader.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-mahabad">Republic of Mahabad</a></li></ul></section>
